## 4.2 Required relationship cardinalities

The data model must support these relationships:

- One report can have many evidence assets.
- One report can have many AI analysis runs over time.
- One AI analysis run can produce many detections.
- One detection belongs to its source image/frame and analysis run.
- One report can contain multiple detections.
- One physical issue can be associated with multiple reports.
- One report can describe more than one physical issue.
- A detection may be linked to zero or one physical issue at a given time, subject to the selected data model.
- One report can have multiple location observations over time.
- One physical issue can have multiple location observations but only one explicitly selected authoritative location at a time under the configured policy.
- One physical issue can have multiple assessment versions.
- One physical issue can have many workflow events and assignments over time.
- One heatmap cell can summarize many eligible physical issues without becoming an issue itself.

## 4.3 Identifier rules

Use persistent identifiers created by the authoritative backend or trusted identity service.

Do not:

- Generate random IDs in a rendering component and treat them as persistent.
- Reuse a report ID as a detection ID.
- Reuse a detection ID as a physical issue ID.
- Derive identity solely from a category and rounded coordinate.
- Change an ID after a report has been referenced by another record.
- Use visible display numbers as database primary keys.

Public-facing reference IDs may be human-friendly, but they must map to stable internal identifiers.

## 4.4 Deletion and retention rules

Do not physically delete a report or its evidence merely because it is rejected, resolved or duplicated.

Use lifecycle state, archival, retention policy and authorized deletion processes. If privacy law or a documented retention policy requires deletion or redaction, implement it deliberately and preserve any legally permitted non-identifying audit trace.

