# PART 8 — REPORT AND ISSUE COUNTING RULES

## 8.1 Define every count

Never show an ambiguous label such as “Total Issues” if the query actually counts raw submissions or AI detections.

Use explicit metrics such as:

- Total reports submitted.
- Total evidence assets.
- Total AI analysis runs.
- Total accepted detections.
- Total unique physical issues.
- Total confirmed independent reports.
- Total suspected duplicate submissions.
- Total verified issues.
- Total unresolved issues.
- Total resolved issues.
- Total reopened issues.
- Total unlocated reports.
- Total pending verification.

Each metric must have a precise query definition and a clear inclusion/exclusion policy.

## 8.2 Multiple detections in one report

If one report contains four pothole detections, it may contribute:

- One report submission.
- Four accepted detections.
- One to four physical issues depending on valid issue association.
- One report-level GPS observation unless additional per-object locations exist.

Do not assume these counts are equal.

## 8.3 Duplicate reports

If five submissions are confirmed to refer to one physical issue, the platform may show:

- Five related reports.
- One unique physical issue.
- The validated number of independent contributing reporters.
- The number of duplicate submissions.

Do not count all five as five unique issues.

Do not count suspected duplicates as confirmed duplicates.

## 8.4 Category totals

A report may contain both pothole and garbage detections. Category totals may therefore refer to detection counts or issue counts, but must be labelled accordingly.

Do not force every report to exactly one category if the AI analysis supports multiple classes.

## 8.5 Resolved and unresolved metrics

Use the authoritative issue status and status history. Do not derive resolution merely from the presence of a resolution note or a hidden map marker.

For “resolved versus unresolved,” define how reopened issues are counted. Prefer current-state counts for current dashboard summaries and preserve the event history for trend charts.

---

# PART 9 — MAP, HEATMAP AND APPLICATION INTEGRATION

## 9.1 Map features must be derived from validated records

Map data must not be created directly from arbitrary UI arrays or raw AI text.

Use a shared map-data adapter or service that consumes:

- Physical issue identity.
- Validated location observation.
- Current authoritative severity assessment.
- Current priority assessment.
- Category.
- Verification state.
- Operational issue status.
- Report and corroboration counts.
- Timestamps and location precision.

## 9.2 Pinpoint map rules

Follow Prompt 2 exactly for point feature types and GPS precision.

Where individual physical issue coordinates are not available, use a clearly named report-observation point or approximate-location point. Do not imply object-level precision where only report-level GPS exists.

Every marker must link to its source issue/report and open the correct detail context.

## 9.3 Heatmap rules

Follow Prompt 2 for heatmap aggregation and cell traceability.

Default severity-cell colour must not be based only on raw report count or average severity. The configured maximum-severity rule must ensure a critical issue is not hidden by many low-severity issues. Show report/issue density as a separate measure.

Each heatmap cell must disclose:

- Aggregation resolution or cell size.
- Filters applied.
- Number of unique physical issues.
- Number of related reports, if relevant.
- Severity distribution.
- Highest severity tier represented.
- The issue IDs contributing to the cell, accessible through authorized drill-down.
- Data freshness timestamp.

## 9.4 Dashboard map and Map Intelligence page

- Home dashboard map: marker-first presentation by default.
- Map Intelligence page: full pinpoint/heatmap mode switch.
- Both must open the same issue details and use the same filters where applicable.
- Selecting a report in the report feed should focus or highlight its corresponding map feature when it has a valid location.
- Selecting a map feature should open the correct report or issue detail, not an unrelated list item.

## 9.5 Filters must have shared semantics

Where equivalent filters are available, define their behavior once:

- Category.
- Severity.
- Priority.
- Verification state.
- Operational issue status.
- Date range.
- Location/ward where authoritative boundary data exists.
- Search term.
- Report source.

The pinpoint and heatmap modes must not interpret the same filter differently without making that difference explicit.

## 9.6 Map data freshness

Display whether the displayed map data is:

- Current.
- Updating.
- Stale.
- Partially available.
- Unavailable.

Do not claim real-time operation if the system uses periodic polling or delayed updates. Label the actual refresh behavior.

## 9.7 Map failure isolation

If the map provider or tile service fails:

- Do not delete report data.
- Keep the list/report workflows usable.
- Show an honest map-unavailable state.
- Preserve the selected filters and issue context where possible.

If the heatmap calculation fails, pinpoint mode should still work when its data is available. If pinpoint rendering fails, the underlying reports and analytics must remain usable.

---

# PART 10 — ANALYTICS AND METRIC CONSISTENCY

## 10.1 One definition for each KPI

The same KPI must not be calculated differently on the Overview page, Analytics page, reports page and priority page.

Create shared query/service functions or versioned metric definitions.

For each metric document:

- Name.
- Definition.
- Entity being counted.
- Filters.
- Status inclusion/exclusion.
- Date field used.
- Duplicate policy.
- Null-data policy.
- Refresh behavior.

## 10.2 Example metric definitions

Use these as a starting point, adapting only to the actual data model:

- **Total Reports:** Count distinct valid report IDs submitted within the selected period.
- **Unique Physical Issues:** Count distinct valid physical issue IDs under the selected scope.
- **Pothole Issues:** Count unique physical issues whose authoritative class is `POTHOLE`.
- **Garbage/Waste Issues:** Count unique physical issues whose authoritative class is `GARBAGE_OR_WASTE`.
- **Pending Verification:** Count reports/issues in the configured human-review state, not merely AI recommendations.
- **Critical Issues:** Count unique physical issues with current authoritative severity `CRITICAL`.
- **High-Priority Issues:** Count eligible physical issues with current priority label `HIGH` or `CRITICAL`, according to the priority scheme.
- **Resolved Issues:** Count physical issues whose current operational state is `RESOLVED`.
- **Reopened Issues:** Count physical issues whose current state is `REOPENED` while preserving previous resolution events.

Do not reuse one count for different metric labels.

## 10.3 Time-series analytics

Define whether a chart uses:

- Submission date.
- Issue first-reported date.
- Verification date.
- Assignment date.
- Resolution date.

Do not mix date semantics inside one trend chart.

Use timezone-aware timestamps and a consistent display timezone. For Tumakuru-facing operations, display local time appropriately, while preserving canonical stored timestamps and offsets according to the backend standard.

## 10.4 Empty and incomplete analytics

If no data exists, display an honest empty state. Do not generate a plausible-looking chart from random values.

If part of the data is unavailable, label the metric partial or unavailable rather than showing a normal value without qualification.

