# PART 19 — PERFORMANCE AND SCALABILITY

## 19.1 Do not over-fetch

Load the data required for the visible page and filter state. Do not fetch every report and every image into the browser merely to display a map.

Use pagination, query limits, lazy image loading and server-side filtering where appropriate.

## 19.2 Avoid N+1 queries

Batch or join related report, issue, assessment, location and verification data where supported.

Inspect query plans for high-volume map and dashboard queries.

## 19.3 Map performance

- Use marker clustering at lower zoom levels.
- Use server-side spatial filtering for large datasets where appropriate.
- Avoid rendering thousands of individual DOM markers when a canvas/WebGL or clustered solution is more suitable.
- Recompute heatmap cells only when source data or relevant filters change.
- Cache derived data with clear invalidation/version rules.
- Keep cell drill-down paginated if many issues contribute.

## 19.4 Analytics performance

Use aggregate queries, materialized views or cached summaries where justified.

Ensure cached results are invalidated by relevant source events.

Do not let a dashboard query load all raw evidence assets or image blobs.

## 19.5 AI job performance

Keep long-running model inference out of short request/response handlers when the provider or runtime requires background processing.

Expose the job state to the application rather than making the user wait indefinitely for a synchronous request.

## 19.6 Resource limits

Set sensible limits for:

- Upload size.
- Number of images per report.
- Video duration and frame extraction count where applicable.
- AI inference attempts.
- Query page size.
- Map viewport result count.
- Export size.

Use configured values, not arbitrary unbounded processing.

